{"body": "The next inquiry I would warrant is a bearing question about the criticism that produced J*, not another repair of J*. The relevant original occurrences are the P9 excerpt on intervals and correlations and the criticize occurrence’s claim that “P9’s wording does fix the object of the objection: intervals.” The uncertainty is whether P9’s sentence “That gives an interval, but intervals lose correlations” fixes an object independently of any account’s claim strength, or whether it becomes an objection to an interval only once one already distinguishes an interval used as a bound from an interval used as a feasibility claim. J* adopts the latter distinction and separates the object of P9’s objection from its P7 target category. But the criticism’s bearing depends on that reading.\n\nIf P9’s wording does fix the object as interval, then the criticism bears, and J*’s object/target and claim-strength refinements are warranted as a correction. If P9’s wording does not by itself distinguish bound from feasibility, then the criticism is itself adding that distinction, and J’s original “independence assumption” is not simply a mislocation but one reading among others. A third outcome is that P9 targets interval-based representation as such; then even a bound claim does not escape the objection, and the next change would be to the global output—set of possible worlds, compatibility relation, or resource vector—rather than to a caveat on claim strength.\n\nThese outcomes change how J* may be used. In the first, J* can be used as a corrected successor: target register T records object and target, while claim layer R distinguishes bound, feasibility, and decision-grade claims. In the second, J* should be presented as one interpretive proposal, not as an established correction, and the criticism’s localization remains contested. In the third, the account needs a different aggregation or constraint representation, because an interval caveat is insufficient.\n\nThe claims and uses that should remain distinguishable are: P7’s rule-versus-record distinction; accepting or rejecting a rule versus accepting or rejecting data; P9’s cross-booking incompatibility as a conditional possibility, not an observation; local alternatives, aggregation, bounds, and correlation objections as different roles; exposing alternatives versus objecting to downstream resolution; J’s five layers; J*’s object/target and claim-strength refinements; and the E009 occurrence as a criticism artifact rather than warehouse evidence.\n\nI am not certifying the criticism’s bearing before asking whether it bears. That is the successor problem. If the frozen text cannot settle that bearing, the next allocation of attention should go to the missing observation: what would count as evidence that a shared resource or vector capacity exists, and what downstream decisions require a feasible set rather than a conservative summary. Without such an observation, the target of P9’s objection remains conditional, and J* remains a proposal about how to record that condition rather than a demonstrated repair.", "commitments": "The next inquiry I would warrant is a bearing question about the criticism that produced J*, not another repair of J*. The relevant original occurrences are the P9 excerpt on intervals and correlations and the criticize occurrence’s claim that “P9’s wording does fix the object of the objection: intervals.” The uncertainty is whether P9’s sentence “That gives an interval, but intervals lose correlations” fixes an object independently of any account’s claim strength, or whether it becomes an objection to an interval only once one already distinguishes an interval used as a bound from an interval used as a feasibility claim. J* adopts the latter distinction and separates the object of P9’s objection from its P7 target category. But the criticism’s bearing depends on that reading.\n\nIf P9’s wording does fix the object as interval, then the criticism bears, and J*’